    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d81">

                <pb facs="0086" n="764" xml:id="pg764"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Dayton</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 390.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-08-31"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >August</hi> 31, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have received your three despatches,
                    namely, No. 329, under date of July 30; No. 332, of August 4, and No. 333, of
                    August 5.</p>
                <p>Under the uniform aspect of our domestic affairs, the matters presented by these
                    papers may safely pass unnoticed.</p>
                <p>You will perceive that the course of events in Mexico is giving rise to much
                    speculation, as well in this country as in Europe, and this speculation takes a
                    direction which may well deserve the consideration of the Emperor’s government,
                    for it indicates a disposition in some quarters to produce alienation between
                    this country and France. This government has said nothing upon the subject,
                    except what is contained in a previous communication made by me to yourself, and
                    it lends no materials or encouragement to the debate to which I have
                    referred.</p>
                <p>I have told you in a previous despatch that the interests of the United States in
                    Texas are not overlooked. I have now to add that preparations have been made,
                    which, as I trust, will be effectual in establishing the national authority in
                    that State.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">William</hi> L. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dayton</hi>, Esq.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
